# Scanner-first customer UX

Grounded in Nielsen Norman Group research (scanning, inverted pyramid, recognition over recall, progressive disclosure, forms EAS, tabs). See plan: scanner-first site UX.

## Always
1. Gist before detail: locked question → locked answer line → then lists/columns/calculators. No filler. No color-on-answer as the scan bait.
2. Prefer controls that look like their job over instructional headings (“Your details”, “Select banks…”). Recognition, not teaching.
3. Bank select: tap bank name; clear selected-row state. Calculated figures: separate treatment (dotted underline / distinct color), never the same click as select. **No checkbox column.**
4. In-page tabs: **browser-tab feel** — active = filled rounded pill + heavier weight; inactive = text only, muted, no fill. Never underline-only.
5. Value before empty-form friction on Explore Banks (sample figures prefilled; Compare banks only). Do not hide “does this work?” behind typing.
6. Preserve meaning; change order and affordance only.

## Never
- Never rely on underline-only for the only active-tab signal.
- Never teach the UI with a sentence when a control can show the action.
- Never make the whole data cell mean both “select bank” and “explain math”.
- Never add fields that fail EAS (Eliminate / Automate / Simplify) without a clear now-needed reason.
